--   Definitions for Affine Birman-Wenzl-Murakami Algebras and Tangles in the Solid Torus
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Affine Birman-Wenzl-Murakami Algebras and Tangles in the Solid Torus* (arXiv:math/0411155) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `Ground`: Ordinary ground data, Definition 2.6, PDF p.7 and §3.1, PDF p.15. Neither z nor any loop parameter is required to be invertible.
--   - `BMWIndex`: One-based generator range, Definitions 3.1 and 3.6, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `BMWAdjacent`: Both adjacency choices in i±1, Definitions 3.1 and 3.6, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `BMWGenerator`: Generators of the ordinary (aff=false) or affine (aff=true) presentation, PDF pp.15,17. At level zero there are no letters; ordinary level one is empty.
--   - `bmwFreeG`: Free g_i, or its inverse if inv=true; zero outside 1≤i<n, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `bmwFreeE`: Free e_i; zero outside 1≤i<n, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `bmwFreeX`: Free x_1 or x_1 inverse, present only at positive affine levels, PDF p.17.
--   - `OrdinaryAffineBMWRel`: Exactly the seven ordinary relation groups (Definition 3.1, PDF p.15) and, when aff=true, the eight affine groups (Definition 3.6, PDF p.17). The q argument specifies every positive q_r; q_0 is unused. There is no cyclotomic relation or admissibility condition. Adjacent index pairs are ordered.
--   - `OrdinaryAffineBMW`: Presented ordinary or affine BMW algebra, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `presentation`: Canonical quotient map, Definitions 3.1 and 3.6, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `ghCrossing`: Source g_i or its inverse, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `ghContraction`: Source e_i, PDF pp.15,17.
--   - `bmwX`: Source x_1 or its inverse, Definition 3.6, PDF p.17.
--   - `forward`: Ordered product h_a ... h_(a+l-1); empty product is 1, PDF p.18.
--   - `backward`: Ordered product in the reverse order of forward, PDF p.18.
--   - `signedPower`: Positive powers use u, negative powers use its specified inverse v, PDF pp.19--21. This avoids the junk-value inverse operation on a general ring.
--   - `fWord`: Recursive f_k word, Definition 5.22, PDF p.40. The offset is a shift of generator indices; f_0=1 extends the empty word convention.
--   - `diamondColumn`: The j-th diamond column e_(k-j) e_(k-j+2) ... e_(k+j), PDF p.40. Only j<k is used.
--   - `diamondWord`: Diamond columns in forward then reverse order, omitting the repeated middle column, Proposition 5.24, PDF p.40.
--   - `alternatingWord`: Interleaved word z_0 x^r_1 z_1 ... x^r_k z_k, Lemma 3.15, PDF p.21.
--   - `bmwWinding`: x_r and its explicit inverse word, §3.3, PDF p.19. At r=0 the helper is zero; assertions about x_r always have 1≤r≤n.
--   - `windingPower`: Integer bmwWinding powers, §3.4, PDF p.21.
--   - `bmwLevel`: The canonical image of a lower-level algebra in W_n, §3.4, PDF pp.21--25. Adjoining the images of its defining generators gives the canonical image, without assuming tower injectivity. sourceAff=false also gives the ordinary subalgebra in an affine ambient algebra.
--   - `fElement`: The f_k word evaluated in W_n with an optional one-step index shift, Definition 5.22 and Proposition 5.26, PDF pp.40--42.
--   - `oddContractions`: Ordered odd contraction word, Definition 5.29, PDF p.44.
--   - `laurent`: All Laurent monomials x_1^mu_1 ... x_n^mu_n, §6.2, PDF p.56.
--   - `rightSpan`: Linear span of U e, §3.4, PDF p.25.
--   - `leftSpan`: Linear span of e U, §5.4, PDF p.44.
--   - `bmwCornerSpan`: Linear span of e U e, §3.4, PDF p.25.
--   - `sandwichSpan`: Linear span of U e U, §5.4, PDF pp.44--45. With U=univ this is the two-sided ideal generated by e, represented as an R-submodule.
--   - `insertionSpan`: Lower-bmwLevel bimodule span U C U, §3.4, PDF p.21.
--   - `translateSpan`: Left translate of a submodule as a linear span, Lemma 3.19, PDF p.25.
